import json
MAD="MadCLIP: Few-shot Medical Anomaly Detection with CLIP"; RECAP="Beyond Static Anchors: Bounded Prototype Conditioning for Language-Free Medical Anomaly Detection"
MLCA="Fine-Tuning CLIP Models with Convolutional Features for Medical Anomaly Detection"
AA="AA-CLIP: Enhancing Zero-Shot Anomaly Detection via Anomaly-Aware CLIP"; PSMP="PSMP-CLIP: Patch-Prompt SAM and Multi-Semantic Prompting for CLIP-Based Zero-Shot Anomaly Detection"
PA=("Leave-one-modality-out zero-shot training on BMAD (RESC and OCT17 held out together); pixel metrics on held-out BrainMRI, LiverCT, RESC; checkpoints and thresholds selected on source validation only; "
    "mask-supervised MVFA re-run under the same rule; 3 seeds, mean and std. ")
PB=("Leave-one-modality-out on BMAD (RESC and OCT17 held out together), MVFA retrained with source-validation selection; K in {2,4,8,16}; 3 seeds x 3 support draws. Baselines: transductive MVFA pipeline, support z-score. ")
O={}
def off(tag,t,s,h,r,m,e,p):
    O[tag]="```json\n"+json.dumps({"Title":t,"Summary":s,"Hypothesis":h,"Rationale":r,"Method":m,"Experiment":e,"Provenance":p},indent=1,ensure_ascii=False)+"\n```"

off("stage3/B1/g2/o5/combination",
 "Two-guarantee scoring: conformal false-positive control plus isotonic-calibrated probabilities for MVFA",
 "Report two numbers per image from the same inductive pipeline: a conformal few-shot p-value that controls false positives on normals, and a probability obtained by passing the log-odds fusion of both branches through an isotonic map learned on source episodes.",
 "On held-out modalities with K >= 4, thresholding the conformal p-value at 0.05 gives a realised false-positive rate within 2 points of 5%, while the isotonic-calibrated fused probability has ECE at least 50% lower than uncalibrated log-odds fusion; image AUROC of the fused score stays within 1 point of the transductive pipeline.",
 "The conformal parent gives a finite-sample false-positive guarantee but its fused log-odds score is calibrated only through parametric source weights; the isotonic parent calibrates monotonically without changing ranking but relies on CDF averages. Clinical deployment needs both a rule that bounds false alarms (a screening threshold) and a calibrated probability (for triage), and they need not come from the same quantity. Combining the parents assigns each guarantee to the component that can provide it: the few-shot conformal p-value for false-positive control and an isotonic map on the log-odds fused score for calibration.",
 "1) Few-shot conformal p-value p as in the conformal parent (LOO support image scores). 2) Zero-shot probability p0 with source-fitted temperature. 3) Fused logit z = w0 logit(p0) + w1 logit(1 - p) + c (logistic weights on source episodes). 4) Isotonic map g fitted on source-episode (z, label) pairs; reported probability g(z). 5) Decision rules: screening = p <= 0.05; triage = g(z). 6) test_few.py: implement both outputs; remove test-set min-max. No adapter training.",
 PB+"Ordered ablation: (i) conformal p only, (ii) + log-odds fusion, (iii) + isotonic calibration. Metrics: realised FPR at p <= 0.05, ECE and Brier of g(z), image AUROC, score shift under resampling. Falsified if realised FPR exceeds 7% or ECE is not halved relative to uncalibrated fusion.",
 [MAD, RECAP, AA])

off("stage3/B1/g2/o1/combination",
 "Level-wise conformal p-values combined with the Cauchy rule and calibrated isotonically",
 "Compute a conformal p-value for each MVFA feature level and branch from leave-one-out support scores, combine the eight dependent p-values with the Cauchy combination test, and map the result to a probability with a source-fitted isotonic map.",
 "Cauchy combination of eight level-wise conformal p-values keeps image AUROC within 1 point of the transductive pipeline, gives a realised false-positive rate within 2 points of the nominal 5% despite the strong dependence between levels, and after isotonic calibration reaches ECE at least 40% lower than the CDF-average parent.",
 "The CDF parent averages branch CDFs, which ignores that averaging dependent ranks is not a valid test; the conformal parent uses a single fused few-shot score and loses level-specific evidence. MVFA's four adapter levels and two branches give eight correlated views of each image. The Cauchy combination test (Liu and Xie, 2020) combines dependent p-values into one whose tail is approximately exact under arbitrary dependence, so level-wise conformal p-values can be combined without modelling their correlation, preserving false-positive control. An isotonic map on the combined statistic then supplies calibrated probabilities.",
 "1) For level l and branch b: LOO support image scores r_i^{bl} (top-1% mean of that level's map); test score s^{bl}; p_bl = (1 + #{r_i >= s})/(K + 1). For the zero-shot branch the support score is the softmax abnormal probability map at that level. 2) Cauchy statistic T = (1/8) sum tan((0.5 - p_bl) pi); combined p = 0.5 - arctan(T)/pi. 3) Isotonic map from -log p to probability fitted on source episodes. 4) test_few.py: implement; remove test-set min-max. No adapter training.",
 PB+"Ordered ablation: (i) single fused conformal p-value, (ii) level-wise Cauchy combination, (iii) Fisher combination (assumes independence, expected to be anti-conservative), (iv) + isotonic calibration. Metrics: realised FPR at 0.05, image AUROC, ECE, score shift under resampling. Falsified if Cauchy-combined FPR exceeds 7% or AUROC drops more than 1 point.",
 [MAD, RECAP])

off("stage3/B1/g2/o3/inspiration",
 "Weighted conformal scoring that borrows source-modality normals to stabilise small support sets",
 "Enlarge the leave-one-out reference set of the few-shot branch at small K by adding normal source-modality images, weighted by their feature similarity to the target supports (weighted conformal prediction), so per-image p-values remain fine-grained and valid under modality shift.",
 "At K = 2 and K = 4, weighted conformal p-values using similarity-weighted source normals reduce the variance of image AUROC across support draws by at least 40% and keep the realised false-positive rate within 3 points of the nominal 5%, whereas unweighted support-only conformal p-values have a resolution of 1/(K + 1) and cannot reach 5% at K = 2.",
 "Both parents rely on the K target supports for their reference distribution, which is too coarse at K = 2 (p-values cannot fall below 1/3). A different mechanism, suggested by the conformal parent's guarantee and the isotonic parent's use of source data, is to borrow normal reference scores from source modalities and correct for the modality shift with likelihood-ratio weights, as in weighted conformal prediction under covariate shift (Tibshirani et al., 2019). Weights are estimated from how similar each source normal's global feature is to the target supports, so similar source anatomy contributes more. This keeps per-image scoring inductive while fixing the small-K resolution problem that limits both parents.",
 "1) Source reference pool: few-shot scores of 500 normal validation images per source modality, each scored against the target support bank (as a target image would be). 2) Weights: w_j = exp(-||g_j - g_supp||^2/(2 h^2)) with g the mean CLS feature at level 4, g_supp the mean of target supports, h = median pairwise distance; support LOO scores get weight 1 (normalised so their total weight equals that of the source pool when K >= 8, and 25% when K = 2). 3) Weighted p-value p = (sum_{i: r_i >= s} w_i + w_test)/(sum_i w_i + w_test). 4) Combine with the zero-shot branch in log-odds as in the conformal parent. 5) test_few.py: implement; remove min-max. No adapter training.",
 PB+"Conditions: support-only conformal; unweighted source pool; weighted source pool. Metrics: realised FPR at p <= 0.05 per K, variance of AUROC across support draws, image AUROC, score shift under resampling. Falsified if weighting does not reduce AUROC variance by 40% at K <= 4 or if realised FPR exceeds 8%.",
 [MAD, RECAP, AA])

off("stage3/B1/g2/o6/combination",
 "Episode-free log-odds fusion: conformal few-shot evidence and calibrated zero-shot probability weighted by support reliability",
 "Fuse the temperature-calibrated zero-shot probability and the conformal few-shot p-value in log-odds with weights set from the target supports themselves (inverse leave-one-out variance of each branch), removing the dependence on source episodes for the fusion.",
 "Support-reliability weights match source-episode-fitted fusion weights within 0.5 image-AUROC points on held-out modalities while eliminating the episode-fitting step, and the fused score keeps the realised false-positive rate within 2 points of the nominal 5% at K >= 4.",
 "The log-odds parent and the conformal parent both need source episodes to fit fusion weights, which assumes that the relative reliability of the two branches transfers across modalities, the very assumption a leave-one-modality-out evaluation questions. Their combination can keep the calibrated zero-shot probability and the conformal few-shot p-value while estimating branch reliability on the target: a branch whose leave-one-out scores on the support normals vary less is more reliable for this modality. Inverse-variance weighting is the standard estimator for combining noisy evidence and needs no abnormal data.",
 "1) Zero-shot: p0 with source-fitted temperature (unchanged). 2) Few-shot: conformal p from LOO support scores. 3) On the K supports, compute LOO logits l0_i = logit(p0_i) and l1_i = logit(1 - p_i) (with p_i from the remaining supports); weights w_b = 1/Var_i(l_b,i), normalised to sum 2. 4) Fused logit z = w0 logit(p0) + w1 logit(1 - p) - median_i(w0 l0_i + w1 l1_i) (centres support normals at 0). 5) Threshold at the 95th percentile of support LOO fused scores. 6) test_few.py: implement; remove min-max. No adapter training.",
 PB+"Conditions: equal weights; support-reliability weights; source-episode weights (reference). Metrics: image AUROC, realised FPR at the support threshold, ECE, score shift under resampling. Falsified if support weights lose more than 0.5 AUROC points relative to episode weights or realised FPR exceeds 7%.",
 [AA, PSMP, MAD])

off("stage3/A1/g2/o3/combination",
 "Agreement-gated edge-aware pseudo-masks: guided-filter scores voted in superpixels, disagreements ignored",
 "Refine continuous patch pseudo-mask scores with a guided filter, vote them within SLIC superpixels, label pixels where both refinements agree, and ignore pixels where they disagree when training MVFA's segmentation branch.",
 "Pixels on which guided-filter and superpixel refinements agree have at least 10 points higher pseudo-label precision than either refinement alone, and training MVFA with disagreement pixels ignored recovers at least 92% of the mask-supervised pixel AUROC on held-out masked modalities, at least 2 points more than either refinement alone.",
 "Guided filtering transfers edges smoothly but can bleed across weak edges; superpixel voting snaps hard to strong edges but errs when a superpixel straddles a lesion boundary. Their errors occur in different places, so agreement between them identifies reliable pixels and disagreement marks exactly the uncertain boundary zone. Using agreement as the target and ignoring disagreement keeps both parents' edge-awareness while removing their individual failure modes, at negligible extra cost.",
 "1) Continuous patch score u from the label-restricted rule (source-validation percentiles), bilinearly upsampled. 2) G = guidedFilter(raw image, u, r = 8, eps = 1e-3) > 0.5. 3) S = SLIC (n_segments 200) majority vote of u > 0.5. 4) Target y = 1 where G and S are both 1, y = 0 where both 0, ignore otherwise; normal images all 0. 5) train_zero.py: masked Focal + Dice over non-ignored pixels; det branch unchanged. 6) Parameters selected on source validation where source masks exist. MVFA hyper-parameters otherwise.",
 PA+"Conditions: true masks; guided only; SLIC only; agreement with ignore; agreement without ignore (disagreement set to 0). Metrics: pseudo-label precision/recall, trained pixel AUROC, Dice, boundary F1. Falsified if agreement precision is not 10 points higher or the trained model does not beat both single refinements by 2 points.",
 [MLCA])
json.dump(O, open("logs/answers/run2_qualsg/s3_g2_ops.json","w"), indent=1, ensure_ascii=False); print(len(O))
